WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.839
I think this is an appropriate moment to try and

00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:27.400
summarize some of the basic concepts behind

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.349
the course, and

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:31.929
actually very much leading on from what

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:33.079
Miroslav, and Doug have

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:37.740
just been saying.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.350
And this starts with a

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.670
a claim, which is that

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.740
to understand the modern world, you have to
be faith-literate.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.029
You have to understand	

00:00:47.029 --> 00:00:51.080
the role of faith.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.830
And it is built on

00:00:53.830 --> 00:01:00.760
what I'm going to call facts, or certain facts,
or certain, I would say,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.100
clear

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.140
and distinguishable

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.570
elements of reality about the modern world.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.420
Globalization

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.159
is growing as a phenomenon,

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:17.940
and it is driven by people,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.240
and it is constantly

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.470
pushing the world together.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.620
There is nowhere-- I

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.830
travel extensively-- there is nowhere I go
in the world today

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:31.700
where it is not 

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.540
obvious that

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.650
the impact of globalization is changing,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.460
not merely the economies, but the societies

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.970
of those countries.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.960
The other thing which, until a few years
ago,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.110
would have been 

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:53.160
a fact that people would have questioned, is that
faith is not diminishing-- it is in fact growing. 

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.820
Now it is not growing everywhere,

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:58.530
and you could argue that in Europe and in some
parts of the West it is diminishing.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.140
Somewhat, at least. 

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.200
But it is nonetheless

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.490
growing worldwide,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:11.439
and not simply within the religion of Islam.

00:02:11.439 --> 00:02:17.189
Christianity is a great phenomenon in China.

00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:19.479
What comes out of these--

00:02:19.479 --> 00:02:20.649
these facts are, then,

00:02:20.649 --> 00:02:23.969
other realities.

00:02:23.969 --> 00:02:26.699
The interaction between people

00:02:26.699 --> 00:02:28.309
is of a different

00:02:28.309 --> 00:02:29.719
nature

00:02:29.719 --> 00:02:30.999
as a result

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:34.859
of globalization pushing people together. Now
what Miroslav was talking about--

00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:37.520
that the encounter between two is really between four,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.339
is really between six, which I was

00:02:39.339 --> 00:02:42.849
a trifle confused about to begin with, but that
you explained and now I understand exactly

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:44.029
what you mean--

00:02:44.029 --> 00:02:46.739
actually it's a very important point, because

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:48.699
if you're

00:02:48.699 --> 00:02:52.770
with somebody who's from the same village in
Europe, the same faith and the same color

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.169
and the same ethnic background,

00:02:55.169 --> 00:02:57.530
there're a whole series of things that you assume

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:01.359
that you don't have to find out about, you just--
it's very natural to you.

00:03:01.359 --> 00:03:02.459
You put in

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:09.789
a whole lot of difference, diversity-- that
is then a different type of encounter--

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:14.540
an encounter obviously with more
dimensions. So that interaction

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.159
between people

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:17.790
through globalization,

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:18.730
through people of--

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:19.699
interaction between

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:23.139
people of different faiths and cultures

00:03:23.139 --> 00:03:25.229
is qualitatively

00:03:25.229 --> 00:03:27.449
far more

00:03:27.449 --> 00:03:29.749
profound to understand and to cope with

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:35.399
than in previous times.

00:03:35.399 --> 00:03:37.809
It is also correct

00:03:37.809 --> 00:03:39.799
that

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:42.129
people's faith

00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:43.209
can be, as

00:03:43.209 --> 00:03:46.760
we said right in the beginning, it can be a
source of

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.489
identity, and is a source of identity, for sure--
can be a source of identity and opposition

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:55.279
in contradiction to others--

00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:57.399
can therefore result in faith

00:03:57.399 --> 00:03:59.999
giving rise to conflict,

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:02.030
which it does, and we saw

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.090
the role of religion

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.790
is dominant in probably a majority of conflicts

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.879
now in the world.

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:12.869
But also,

00:04:12.869 --> 00:04:14.599
faith can give rise to good works;

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:15.520
that is plainly true

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.409
as well.

00:04:19.409 --> 00:04:21.000
At the same time,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.729
there are 

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:25.780
differences between faiths, and also--

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.699
as Doug was just saying-- there is some

00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:30.159
point at which

00:04:30.159 --> 00:04:31.539
there seems to be

00:04:31.539 --> 00:04:32.960
a rapprochement

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:39.210
of values between the different faiths.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.469
We're just dealing with facts now; these are the
things that go to make up this 

00:04:43.469 --> 00:04:48.669
matrix of-- what I would call the matrix
of realities around these issues to do with faith and

00:04:48.669 --> 00:04:50.119
globalization.

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:53.789
There are also -- this is another the thing
that is happening in the world at the moment--

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:57.650
there are debates within each state.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:04:59.979
So one of the things that is

00:04:59.979 --> 00:05:02.250
really 

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:03.860
mistaken is to talk about

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.330
a faith as if there is a certain

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.790
dominant view within that faith of how it
sees the world. Actually, you look at any

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.229
major faith today,

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:15.740
there is plainly a traditional and a modernizing
tendency;

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.599
there is a reaching out tendency and a

00:05:19.599 --> 00:05:22.219
reaching in tendency--

00:05:22.219 --> 00:05:24.289
this is true within

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:25.720
virtually all faiths, and

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.860
if you take debates of agenda--

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.329
just take debates over gay rights--

00:05:32.329 --> 00:05:38.559
you can have people of the same faith reaching
diametrically different conclusions on these issues.

00:05:38.559 --> 00:05:45.559
But faith will be the prism within which
many of these issues are discussed and debated.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.769
Here's something else that I think is important:

00:05:50.769 --> 00:05:54.369
is that faith communities themselves-- they're also
evolving.

00:05:54.369 --> 00:05:58.490
Now they're evolving off in different directions.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.189
And you can get

00:06:00.189 --> 00:06:02.259
even contradictory directions.

00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:05.800
But that is also happening.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.409
So my sort of stopping point is:

00:06:10.409 --> 00:06:11.419
you need to be

00:06:11.419 --> 00:06:12.259
faith-literate.

00:06:12.259 --> 00:06:14.460
Why? Because

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.140
globalization is pushing people together,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.949
the world of faith is growing,

00:06:17.949 --> 00:06:21.110
the interplay between people then becomes
more important,

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:24.199
faith can be a force for good-- it can be a
force for bad--

00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:26.169
there are clearly

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:27.650
common areas within--

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.680
between faiths, there are major differences--

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.349
but also, there is this

00:06:32.349 --> 00:06:35.989
evolution within the faith community

00:06:35.989 --> 00:06:37.850
where there are differences between

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:40.050
people as to what they think

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.620
within the same faith-- intra-faith--

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.289
and the faith communities themselves are 
evolving.

00:06:46.289 --> 00:06:48.760
So what this means, in my view, and one of 

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:49.750
the things-- 'cause

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:53.910
all the way through, what I'm trying to think of is what are
the practical consequences

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.179
for policy, for thought, as a result of
this?

00:06:58.179 --> 00:07:02.269
What is obvious is that this relationship between
the world of faith and the world of globalization,

00:07:02.269 --> 00:07:06.089
is an important part of understanding the 
modern world and

00:07:06.089 --> 00:07:06.710
having

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:11.160
a relationship that is positive between those
two is an important part of making the modern 

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.280
world work. 

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.099
Now one of the

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:16.399
practical consequences out of this, I think,

00:07:16.399 --> 00:07:17.409
is that we need

00:07:17.409 --> 00:07:19.650
to develop

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.309
actually, a far greater

00:07:22.309 --> 00:07:26.909
understanding of what these-- how these
different forces are developing,

00:07:26.909 --> 00:07:29.679
and this is not a religious question.

00:07:29.679 --> 00:07:32.060
One of the reasons why this course is a--

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:33.680
is a collaboration

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.990
between the School of Management and the School 
of Divinity is precisely because we recognize this

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:41.989
is not just a religious question; it has major
social, economic, and political

00:07:41.989 --> 00:07:43.330
consequences.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:44.970
So the question that we then

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.610
ask, arising out of this, is--

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:48.620
okay, so

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.509
how do we make--

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:53.939
how do we make this world work?

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:55.389
And

00:07:55.389 --> 00:07:59.860
I would suggest that the following is essential
if we want to make it work--

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:03.389
here are five points I'll make briefly.

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:05.240
The first is, we need knowledge.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.740
We need to have knowledge.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.180
Knowledge is essential.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:09.659
Knowledge is good in itself--

00:08:09.659 --> 00:08:11.580
an explanation-- knowing--

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.909
being able to understand the other person.

00:08:13.909 --> 00:08:15.999
This is a central part of it.

00:08:15.999 --> 00:08:19.569
So one major aspect of what we have to do
is to make sure that people have the

00:08:19.569 --> 00:08:21.069
ability and capacity 

00:08:21.069 --> 00:08:23.969
and opportunity

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:25.809
to learn. 

00:08:25.809 --> 00:08:27.669
Ignorance, to fear, to conflict

00:08:27.669 --> 00:08:31.539
is one very obvious

00:08:31.539 --> 00:08:33.410
train of happenstance. 

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:35.000
Understanding,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.100
acceptance, peace is another.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.380
So. The fact that you know about the other person,
the fact that you understand where they

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.210
come from, the fact that you have some

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:46.710
concept in your mind of their cultural background--
doesn't mean to say you get on, but

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.470
it's obviously a help and I would say that knowledge
is essential.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:50.710
Essential

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:53.210
at the level of leaders and nations,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.410
which is why things like--

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.780
this concept that Turkey and Spain launched
about the alliance of civilizations, which

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.750
is very much-- obviously a sort of counterpoint
to the Huntington thesis--

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.710
it's important at that level.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.880
It is important to the level of action and
I

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.220
agree with what

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:16.220
Doug is saying, and actually the discussion with
Rick about

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:19.710
hand to heart to head came out of discussions 

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.460
we had had with Rick and with others,

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.689
which is that if you start with action,

00:09:24.689 --> 00:09:28.520
then you're more likely to get people

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.180
to interact together

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.010
without a lot of complicating theological

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.519
questions where you immediately highlight
the differences between people.

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:41.059
So for example one part of what we do in
in the foundation is to try and get people

00:09:41.059 --> 00:09:45.420
to work together on the anti-malaria campaign.

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.070
However--

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:50.329
and this may seem strange from me since I come
from the world of the sort of--

00:09:50.329 --> 00:09:53.920
it's supposed to be the world of practice, in
politics,

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.160
and you guys come from the world of academia--

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:01.020
I also think, though, that

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.550
education--

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:07.630
not just in the terms of the exchange of information, 
but in the academic sense-- is an important

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:11.560
part of this. I think we need to understand
some of the phenomena that are working here;

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.200
I think we have to have a far greater

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.750
ability both in research, empirical
evidence, and data to try and work out what

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.340
are these different

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:22.740
strands that are going on

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:23.900
within the world today.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:27.030
And so when I say, first point is knowledge
is essential,

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.050
I think there's a massive amount

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:31.100
that we've still got to accomplish

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:34.250
in trying to make that knowledge as deep

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:38.390
and as--

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:40.400
as deep and as

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:41.300
comprehensive

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:44.240
as it needs to be.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:48.390
The second thing-- so that's the first thing:
how do we make this world of 

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:52.930
faith and globalization work? Knowledge. The second

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:54.220
is this:

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.840
you do need to acknowledge, in any society,

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.570
if you're going to have 

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:01.570
a society where there is harmony between faiths--

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:05.970
there's got to be some common space and some
common rules of engagement.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.319
And this is in a sense what we were talking
with Nick

00:11:08.319 --> 00:11:10.470
last week about.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:15.040
You have to accept that different faiths have an
equal right to speak;

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.009
you have to accept that actually

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:23.360
the secular world and the faith world also
have equal rights to speak;

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.380
you have to have some rules of engagement for
this.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:27.830
I think you need--

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:29.900
as we were saying last week-- you need

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:32.250
some civility in the nature of the debate,

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.390
and you also need

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:38.389
countries and societies that are of different
faiths and different cultures--

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:40.119
they've got to have some

00:11:40.119 --> 00:11:42.330
common space where people congregate

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.210
and are prepared to say,

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.360
"This is what we share in common,

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:47.020
and this is

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:50.130
an important part also of our identity."

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:53.430
We don't give up our faith identity,

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.550
but, you know, just as in America when

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:59.630
the flag is there, people rise to salute
the flag, and that is what you do whatever

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:02.800
your faith, whatever your color, whatever your
background--

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.860
that is a unifying force.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:06.000
Actually

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.710
this is something that is

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:09.470
can be seen 

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:12.010
as patriotism, and of course it is,

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:15.180
but actually there's something else that's
really important about that;

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:19.530
it's an indication that at this point and
in this space, everyone comes together.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:21.319
And I think that's a critical part;

00:12:21.319 --> 00:12:23.569
I think it's a critical part of the rules of engagement

00:12:23.569 --> 00:12:26.020
for a multi-faith society.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:31.510
But I think you need that common space and you need
those common rules of engagement.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:34.850
I think thirdly that

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:38.000
in practice

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:39.870
what this then means

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.080
is an attitude

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.029
that is

00:12:43.029 --> 00:12:46.190
what I would call open rather than closed.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:49.890
And what's more, it should be a goal
of public policy

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:55.970
to encourage or facilitate that openness. 

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:57.590
There's an interesting

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.580
side consequence of this,

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:01.920
which is

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:03.689
it actually has an impact

00:13:03.689 --> 00:13:05.290
on globalization as well-- on

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:07.750
your economic policy.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.740
Because it's very hard

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:12.910
to have a society that is open

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:16.699
in terms of faith, in terms of culture, in terms of
background,

00:13:16.699 --> 00:13:18.980
if it's closed economically.

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:21.410
So there is a link, in fact, between

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:24.630
the idea that in the modern world

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:26.319
human capital is essential,

00:13:26.319 --> 00:13:28.209
that human capital should be

00:13:28.209 --> 00:13:29.220
on the basis

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.340
of

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.890
the equal dignity of each human being

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:36.160
and actually a successful economy,

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:40.570
I hope, where people are recognizing that they
live in a world

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:41.970
in which there's going to be

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:45.490
trade between nations and exchange of

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:47.530
intellectual,

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:51.510
human, and financial capital.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:55.610
And actually, one of the reasons why

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:59.430
protectionism, in my view, is not a good response
in today's world is

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:03.700
partly because if you take that closed economic
attitude, it actually becomes quite hard to have

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:04.869
an open

00:14:04.869 --> 00:14:05.449
attitude to

00:14:05.449 --> 00:14:07.060
people of different faiths

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:11.220
and backgrounds within society.

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:13.279
So, in practice, it requires an open

00:14:13.279 --> 00:14:17.050
and not a closed attitude-- that should
be a goal of public policy--

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:21.100
and here's one other thing which I think is really
important--

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.950
is that if you have that open attitude,

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:28.140
then I think

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.780
it doesn't really work

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:32.610
unless

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:34.350
it comes on the basis of that

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.410
openness being there available

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:39.900
for all and not simply for a few.

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:41.610
So there is something universal

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.140
about that

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:48.280
for it to work.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:52.050
Now, fourthly,

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:56.840
even if you believe you have an exclusive
truth-- faith truth--

00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:03.230
you've still got to be in support of 
an inclusive society.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:07.650
This is a point that Miroslav has often made and
I think it's

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:09.859
worth just spending a moment on, which is that

00:15:09.859 --> 00:15:15.350
what he says, in essence-- as I understand it-- is this: that

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:16.980
if you have a faith 

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:17.459
and--

00:15:17.459 --> 00:15:20.040
let's see--

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:24.390
you're a Christian, you believe in salvation
through Jesus Christ--

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.370
you cannot at a certain point--

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:29.820
I mean that is what you believe is the
truth--

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:33.370
you cannot at a certain point say, "Well,

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.990
but I think you who don't believe that

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:38.200
have equal validity, because--

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:39.750
I mean, obviously that's

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.700
the whole essence of your belief. 

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:47.870
And so Miroslav, I think, has drawn a distinction between, 
as it were,

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:49.360
exclusivity in terms of,

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.640
"This is my truth, my faith truth that I believe in,"

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:53.450
and then 

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:59.090
using that exclusivity in terms of shutting
out the other person, right?

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:01.890
And I think that is true, but I also think

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:03.100
something else

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:07.390
that is interesting about the way globalization

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:08.480
interplays with

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:11.060
faith

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:12.250
is that

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:15.040
as that--

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:18.960
as that knowledge deepens,

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:22.610
and as

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:24.040
you learn

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:28.650
to inhabit freely that common space,

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:34.160
and as that produces what I would say is a kind of
more open attitude,

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.940
I think

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:40.660
faith also evolves in that process.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:41.590
So that--

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:43.400
so that

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:44.939
yes, you still believe

00:16:44.939 --> 00:16:46.209
in your own faith

00:16:46.209 --> 00:16:47.740
but your

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:54.740
openness to someone of another faith

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:59.410
is more than just accepting that they have
that faith

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:01.920
and that they believe it's valid. 

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:07.380
You also start to accept their spiritual
sincerity, right, 

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:10.700
even if you don't necessarily accept their truth
claim.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:12.660
And that's quite important,

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:15.840
and you were quoting St. Paul 

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:18.250
earlier-- the extraordinary thing about the early Christian church

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:20.160
was St. Paul

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:23.760
going out and saying, "This is a message that
I have for

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.730
Greek as well as Jew"-- that

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:31.480
that this is, as it were, a message that is
universal and not simply restricted

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:34.600
to the Jewish people.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:36.710
When you come to Islam,

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:41.900
the whole point about what the Prophet was
saying was a way actually to push it even

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:44.740
broader

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:49.380
than, up to then, either the Jewish faith or
the Christian faith had acknowledged.

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:53.470
My point is simply this:

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:55.900
that the fourth thing that I think you require

00:17:55.900 --> 00:18:00.490
for

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:04.780
this world of faith and globalization
to work--

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.410
is that at least

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:07.440
you--

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:11.970
even if you believe you have the exclusive
truth, you've got to want an inclusive society.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.890
But I also think that

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:18.280
you need to accept

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:21.460
that there is a level of respect

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:24.510
that you have for the person of a different faith

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:29.010
that is more than simply accepting as a
fact that they have a different faith,

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:34.240
but you also accept the sincerity with which
they hold that faith, and that

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:37.120
some good can come out of it.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:38.909
In other words-- you know, when I 

00:18:38.909 --> 00:18:40.770
engage with somebody

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:44.980
of a different faith who's engaged with us
in our projects-- for example in Africa, on the

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:47.140
anti-malaria campaign, we work with--

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:49.870
I mean, primarily, actually 

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:52.960
with Jews and Muslims as well as Christians,

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.540
although actually there are

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:56.440
Hindus involved

00:18:56.440 --> 00:19:00.460
in it as well, but--

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:01.800
when I see what a

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.170
difference,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.050
let's say,

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:06.700
somebody of

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:09.710
the Jewish faith-- what a difference their faith
makes

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:13.130
to them and their actions,

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:15.840
it's more than, for me, simply,

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.480
"Well, I accept that they happen to feel very strongly,

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:20.510
and I'm prepared to accept that reality."

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:22.980
I'm actually accepting something more than
that.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.080
I'm accepting

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:26.750
that there is a

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:30.080
sincerity about their view,

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:30.490
and

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:37.490
a motivation as a result of that for them
to do good that

00:19:38.679 --> 00:19:43.580
sits alongside, if you like, the issue to do
with who is right and wrong in a faith sense.

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:46.380
It sits alongside it quite powerfully.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:47.530
You what I mean?

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:49.280
So it's a--

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:54.260
and this is what I think is fascinating
about the way the world then develops, because

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:59.390
the-- yes, you keep your own religious faith, and
you keep that strongly,

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:01.559
but your very open to the other

00:20:01.559 --> 00:20:07.790
and you're working alongside them and you're understanding
why it is that they are as they are--

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:08.840
also

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.460
changes your own sense

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.980
of what faith

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:14.279
is about. It doesn't 

00:20:14.279 --> 00:20:15.940
change your own belief, 

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:20.150
but it does let you acknowledge someone of
a different belief, I think in a far more profound

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:20.870
way

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:24.520
than simply accepting as a matter of fact
they've got a different faith. 

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.400
And that's where you move, I think,

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:28.510
from tolerance to respect--

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:34.039
which is a very, very important part of the
journey.

00:20:34.039 --> 00:20:36.650
And the final thing

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:38.780
is this--

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:42.050
and here is where I think

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:43.639
the debate is, in a sense,

00:20:43.639 --> 00:20:45.270
at its most 

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:46.210
interesting-- because

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.159
we've really discussed this very often,

00:20:48.159 --> 00:20:50.120
for obvious reasons,

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:52.340
in the context of what

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.910
in an era of globalization, how do you make

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.090
religious faith end up as a source of

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:59.680
coexistence and not

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:03.070
conflict. So we really--

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:04.500
we've really looked at

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.660
the issue of faith

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:10.360
more in terms of,

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:15.850
is it a force for good or ill in faith terms,
or in social terms.

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.150
There's something else, though, that is

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:18.880
really interesting--

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.590
which is that globalization

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:23.190
is a force,

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:27.890
but if it is to be welcomed,

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:30.790
it needs for the benefits of that

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:31.110
globalization

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.280
to be shared,

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:35.480
for the opportunities

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:39.740
not to be restricted,

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:41.890
and globalization without values

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:44.530
governing it and underpinning it--

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.060
globalization is less likely

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:47.930
to be accepted,

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:50.210
less likely to be successful,

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:52.830
and actually less likely to work,

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:54.409
because if it's not accepted,

00:21:54.409 --> 00:21:56.530
and it's not successful,

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:58.600
globalization itself can become

00:21:58.600 --> 00:21:59.620
a force

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:02.160
that is-- that can produce conflict

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:05.900
or ill.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:08.700
And therefore, that's why therefore what is interesting

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:09.630
about the role

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.630
of faith and why

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:15.020
people thought maybe you'd squeeze faith out 

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:16.990
of society

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:20.010
as economic prosperity advanced. 

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.120
Why hasn't it happened, in the end?

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:27.480
It hasn't happened because of something
very deep within people

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.100
that says, "I 

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.640
understand

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:32.700
the benefits of globalization,

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:38.490
I desire the material prosperity that
may come with it,

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.380
but I know that in and of itself,

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:41.970
there has to be something more

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:43.570
in order to make life

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:46.000
worth living.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:47.580
And what is more, that

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:52.750
as this globalization, which is in some ways
an impersonal force, changes and disrupts

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.750
people's lives,

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.110
so there is a

00:22:56.110 --> 00:22:58.620
desire to reassert some sense of values,

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:02.380
some sense of shaping this process of globalization,

00:23:02.380 --> 00:23:05.390
to ends that we consider

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:08.700
compatible with progress in humanity.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:09.820
So I think

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:11.190
the interesting thing

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:15.030
in the end, as Miroslav was saying earlier,

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:16.910
and

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:19.950
Doug was remarking about 

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:23.190
the point at which

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:24.980
people of religious faith come to it

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:25.970
a certain

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.750
common understanding--

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:34.100
the question that faith does ask about globalization,
and that should be asked; you don't have to be a

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:35.929
person of faith to ask it, incidentally--

00:23:35.929 --> 00:23:40.500
but it is the question that faith asks of globalization,
is: to what purpose?

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:41.750
What is the end?

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:46.720
What are we trying to achieve here? And

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:48.200
without that,

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:50.330
it might be globalization

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:51.330
is a

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:53.159
poorer and possibly even

00:23:53.159 --> 00:23:54.660
more dangerous

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:56.880
phenomenon. 

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:58.369
So that's my

00:23:58.369 --> 00:24:01.390
view of why this--

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:02.419
this matters, 

00:24:02.419 --> 00:24:04.120
and what we need to do

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:05.100
to make it--

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:06.890
to make it work.

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:12.040
And the conclusion, actually, that you come to
overall from this is,

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:14.780
the relationship between faith and globalization

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:18.520
is fundamentally important

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:20.370
and

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:24.040
in order for it to work,

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:31.150
the world of faith has got something to say
about this process--

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:33.440
not merely from the point of view of how we

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.510
avoid conflict between people of different
faiths,

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:40.760
but actually from the point of view of how
you make this process of globalization humane

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:42.800
and progressive, and

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.010
working for the benefit of all humankind.

